@prefix : <http://www.w3id.org/hsu-aut/RobotCapabilities#> .
@prefix owl: <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdf: <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .
@prefix vann: <http://purl.org/vocab/vann/> .
@prefix CORAX: <http://purl.org/ieee1872-owl/corax#> .
@prefix SOMA: <http://www.ease-crc.org/ont/SOMA.owl#> .
@prefix SRDL: <http://knowrob.org/kb/srdl2-cap.owl#> .
@base <http://www.w3id.org/hsu-aut/RobotCapabilities#> .

<http://www.w3id.org/hsu-aut/RobotCapabilities> rdf:type owl:Ontology ;
    owl:versionIRI <http://www.w3id.org/hsu-aut/RobotCapabilities/2.0.0> ;
    owl:versionInfo "2.0.0" ;
    rdfs:label "RobotCapabilities"@en ;
    dcterms:title "RobotCapabilities - Basic Types of Capabilities of Autonomous Robots"@en ;
    dcterms:description """A small, deliberately shallow taxonomy of the basic kinds of capability an autonomous robot can provide, as types of RobotCapability. The pattern imports nothing: an ontology that uses it places RobotCapability under its own notion of capability with a single axiom.

The cut is not newly invented. It is taken from twelve independent groupings that make the same distinction, among them IEEE 1872 (which types robot parts as sensing, actuating, processing and communicating), IEEE 1872.2 clause 4.4.9 (which gives acquisition of information, physical modification of objects and object classification as examples of functions), the published ontologies SRDL and SOMA, ISO 8373:2021 clause 3.1, SAE JAUS, ASTM F3470-20 and the SPARC Multi-Annual Roadmap. Correspondences to SRDL and SOMA are recorded on each class.

There is no standard that fixes a taxonomy of capabilities of autonomous robots. IDTA 02020 (Capability Description for the Asset Administration Shell) refers to external ontologies for capability types and states that such ontologies are "not yet widely available"; this pattern is meant to be usable there."""@en ;
    dcterms:creator "Luis Miguel Vieira da Silva" ;
    dcterms:publisher "Helmut-Schmidt-University, Institute of Automation Technology" ;
    dcterms:license <https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT> ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-05"^^xsd:date ;
    dcterms:modified "2026-11-09"^^xsd:date ;
    vann:preferredNamespacePrefix "RobotCap" ;
    vann:preferredNamespaceUri "http://www.w3id.org/hsu-aut/RobotCapabilities#" .


#################################################################
#    Annotation properties used in the metadata
#################################################################
# Declared so that the ontology stays within OWL 2 DL: using an undeclared
# annotation property puts the imports closure out of profile.

dcterms:title rdf:type owl:AnnotationProperty .
dcterms:description rdf:type owl:AnnotationProperty .
dcterms:creator rdf:type owl:AnnotationProperty .
dcterms:publisher rdf:type owl:AnnotationProperty .
dcterms:license rdf:type owl:AnnotationProperty .
dcterms:created rdf:type owl:AnnotationProperty .
dcterms:modified rdf:type owl:AnnotationProperty .
vann:preferredNamespacePrefix rdf:type owl:AnnotationProperty .
vann:preferredNamespaceUri rdf:type owl:AnnotationProperty .


#################################################################
#    Annotation properties used for the correspondences
#################################################################

# Used without importing SKOS: the mappings are annotations, and pulling the
# whole SKOS vocabulary into every importing ontology is not worth it.
skos:closeMatch rdf:type owl:AnnotationProperty ;
                rdfs:comment "Used here in its SKOS sense: the two concepts are similar enough to be interchanged in some applications, but not identical. They are not equivalent classes - see the note on the three notions of capability below."@en .


#################################################################
#
#    THREE NOTIONS OF CAPABILITY
#
#    The classes below are types of :RobotCapability, read as a specification
#    of what a robot can do -- the sense the CSS reference model gives the word,
#    "implementation-independent specification of a function". SOMA and SRDL use
#    the word for something else: a disposition of an agent to bring about
#    certain effects. ROSETTA and PMK use it for a property of a skill or of
#    the robot.
#
#    A specification is not a disposition. The specification "transport"
#    exists whether or not any robot has the disposition to carry it out.
#    The correspondences below are therefore skos:closeMatch and deliberately
#    not owl:equivalentClass: equating them would make every capability in
#    this stack a disposition.
#
#################################################################

#################################################################
#    Classes
#################################################################

###  http://www.w3id.org/hsu-aut/RobotCapabilities#RobotCapability
# The root of this pattern. A capability an autonomous robot can provide.
#
# The pattern stands on its own and imports nothing: the nine types below are
# meant to be reusable with any capability model. An ontology that uses one --
# for instance one built on the CSS reference model, where a capability is an
# "implementation-independent specification of a function" -- places this root
# under its own notion of capability. That single axiom belongs in the aligning
# ontology, not here.
:RobotCapability rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "robot capability"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A capability an autonomous robot can provide. Root of this pattern; the nine types below divide it by what the robot does, not by how it is built."@en .


###  http://www.w3id.org/hsu-aut/RobotCapabilities#PerceptionCapability
:PerceptionCapability rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf :RobotCapability ;
    rdfs:label "perception capability"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The specification of a function that acquires information about the environment or about the robot itself. Corresponds to what IEEE 1872.2, 4.4.9 calls the acquisition of information via sensing behavior, and to what IEEE 1872 RPARTS covers with robotSensingPart."@en ;
    skos:closeMatch SOMA:Perceiving , SRDL:PerceptionCapability .


###  http://www.w3id.org/hsu-aut/RobotCapabilities#ProprioceptionCapability
:ProprioceptionCapability rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf :PerceptionCapability ;
    rdfs:label "proprioception capability"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Perception directed at the robot's own state rather than at its environment. Kept apart because the distinction matters in planning: being able to sense the environment is not the same as knowing one's own pose. SAE JAUS assigns pose sensing to mobility rather than to environment sensing, and SOMA separates Proprioceiving from Perceiving."@en ;
    skos:closeMatch SOMA:Proprioceiving .


###  http://www.w3id.org/hsu-aut/RobotCapabilities#LocomotionCapability
:LocomotionCapability rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf :RobotCapability ;
    rdfs:label "locomotion capability"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The specification of a function that changes the location of the robot itself. The constitutive capability of a mobile robot. Present in every grouping examined, and named in ISO 8373:2021, 3.1 alongside manipulation and positioning. The modality - air, ground, water - is deliberately not modelled here; it belongs to the platform of the robot, and modelling it twice would be an error."@en ;
    skos:closeMatch SOMA:Navigating , SRDL:LocomotionCapability .


###  http://www.w3id.org/hsu-aut/RobotCapabilities#ManipulationCapability
:ManipulationCapability rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf :RobotCapability ;
    rdfs:label "manipulation capability"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The specification of a function that changes the location or the state of an object other than the robot itself. Corresponds to the physical modification of objects via force exertion in IEEE 1872.2, 4.4.9, and to robotActuatingPart in IEEE 1872 RPARTS."@en ;
    skos:closeMatch SOMA:Manipulating , SRDL:ObjectManipulationCapability .


###  http://www.w3id.org/hsu-aut/RobotCapabilities#PositioningCapability
:PositioningCapability rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf :ManipulationCapability ;
    rdfs:label "positioning capability"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Bringing an object into a defined position and orientation. ISO 8373:2021, 3.1 names positioning next to locomotion and manipulation; SOMA places Positioning under Actuating."@en ;
    skos:closeMatch SOMA:Positioning .


###  http://www.w3id.org/hsu-aut/RobotCapabilities#ReasoningCapability
:ReasoningCapability rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf :RobotCapability ;
    rdfs:label "reasoning capability"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The specification of a function that derives new information from information already present - classifying, planning, deciding. Corresponds to object classification via reasoning behavior in IEEE 1872.2, 4.4.9, and to robotProcessingPart in IEEE 1872 RPARTS. CSS admits this as a capability because it defines the effect of a capability as being in the physical OR VIRTUAL world. Note that this type is absent from groupings rooted in manufacturing, where planning happens outside the machine; it is needed for autonomous robots, which decide for themselves."@en ;
    skos:closeMatch SOMA:DerivingInformation , SRDL:ComputationalCapability .


###  http://www.w3id.org/hsu-aut/RobotCapabilities#CommunicationCapability
:CommunicationCapability rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf :RobotCapability ;
    rdfs:label "communication capability"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The specification of a function that exchanges information with another agent. Corresponds to robotCommunicatingPart in IEEE 1872 RPARTS. Notably absent from SRDL, which has no communication capability at all."@en ;
    skos:closeMatch SOMA:CommunicationTask .


###  http://www.w3id.org/hsu-aut/RobotCapabilities#HumanRobotCommunicationCapability
:HumanRobotCommunicationCapability rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf :CommunicationCapability ;
    rdfs:label "human-robot communication capability"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Communication with a human. The distinction by partner is taken from IEEE 1872 CORAX rather than newly introduced; this class specifies a function whose execution is a CORAX:HumanRobotCommunication."@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso CORAX:HumanRobotCommunication .


###  http://www.w3id.org/hsu-aut/RobotCapabilities#RobotRobotCommunicationCapability
:RobotRobotCommunicationCapability rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf :CommunicationCapability ;
    rdfs:label "robot-robot communication capability"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Communication with another robot, the precondition for a team to act in a coordinated way. The distinction by partner is taken from IEEE 1872 CORAX."@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso CORAX:RobotRobotCommunication .


#################################################################
#
#    NO DISJOINTNESS - AND WHY
#
#    The basic types are deliberately NOT declared disjoint.
#
#    Capabilities that are composed of several basic kinds are typed with
#    several of them rather than given a class of their own. Localisation,
#    for example, is perception and reasoning at once:
#
#        ex:Localise a RobotCap:PerceptionCapability , RobotCap:ReasoningCapability .
#
#    The same holds for transport (locomotion and manipulation) and for area
#    surveying (locomotion and perception). None of the groupings examined
#    treats these as basic types; SRDL and ASTM F3470-20 do list localisation
#    separately, but it decomposes.
#
#    Keeping the pattern open is what makes the taxonomy shallow and
#    extensible. Declaring the types disjoint would forbid it.
#
#    Application-specific capabilities - transporting, inspecting, guarding -
#    belong in the application ontology of the case at hand, not here: a
#    reusable pattern has to stay independent of the task.
#
#################################################################
